.TH WAKEFUL 1 "2026" "wakeful" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wakeful \- keep a Plasma session awake for a time or while coding agents work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wakeful
[\fB\-\-no\-tray\fR] [\fB\-v\fR]
.br
.B wakeful start
[\fB\-\-screen\fR] [\fIDURATION\fR]
.br
.B wakeful extend
\fIDURATION\fR
.br
.B wakeful stop
.br
.B wakeful agents
[\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR | \fIAGENT\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR |
\fBgrace\fR \fIDURATION\fR]
.br
.B wakeful status
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no command,
.B wakeful
puts an icon in the system tray. Two things can keep the computer awake, on
their own or together: a timer, started from the menu for a preset length, a
custom length, or until it is turned off, then extended or stopped; and the
agentic checks, which keep it awake while a coding agent is working (see
.B CODING AGENTS
below).
.PP
The icon is a closed eye when nothing is enabled, a half\-lidded eye while the
agentic checks are on but nothing is working and no timer runs, and an open eye
while the computer is being kept awake. Left\-clicking it steps through off,
agentic checks, and agentic checks with a timer of the last length used, then
back to off. With no agent ticked the step for agentic checks is left out. The
tooltip shows the time remaining.
.PP
The timer is released when its time runs out and when it is stopped; every
inhibition is released when
.B wakeful
exits or crashes: they are held on its own D\-Bus connection, and the session's
power management drops them when that connection closes.
.PP
With a command,
.B wakeful
asks the running tray to act, which makes it usable from scripts and from a
custom keyboard shortcut.
.SH MODES
The mode chosen in the menu applies to the timer and to the agentic checks; a
timer started with \fBwakeful start\fR uses \fB\-\-screen\fR instead.
.TP
.B Keep awake, screen can turn off
The default. Idle sleep is blocked; the screen still dims, turns off and locks
on its usual timers. Uses org.freedesktop.PowerManagement.Inhibit.
.TP
.B Keep awake and screen on
Also blocks dimming, turning the screen off and idle screen locking. Adds
org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver.Inhibit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBstart\fR [\fB\-\-screen\fR] [\fIDURATION\fR]
Start a timer, replacing any running one. Without a duration it lasts until
stopped. \fB\-\-screen\fR keeps the screen on too.
.TP
\fBextend\fR \fIDURATION\fR
Add time to the running timer.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the timer. The agentic checks carry on.
.TP
\fBagents\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
Turn the agentic checks on or off. Which agents are ticked is kept.
.TP
\fBagents\fR \fIAGENT\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
Tick or untick one agent: \fBclaude\fR, \fBcodex\fR or \fBopencode\fR.
.TP
\fBagents grace\fR \fIDURATION\fR
How long to keep awake after the last working agent stops; \fB0\fR for no
extra time. The default is a minute.
.TP
.BR status ", " agents
Print what is being kept awake and why, and which agent sessions are working.
.PP
\fIDURATION\fR is minutes as a bare number (\fB90\fR), or hours, minutes and
seconds such as \fB1h30m\fR, \fB45m\fR or \fB20s\fR. \fBforever\fR means no end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tray
Run without a tray icon, controlled only through the commands above. The agentic
checks then start off, and neither they nor the grace period are remembered.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log debug detail to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CODING AGENTS
The agentic checks are below the timer choices in the tray's \fBKeep awake
for\fR menu: \fBAgentic checks enabled\fR, and a checkmark for each of Claude
Code, Codex and opencode. While they are on, the computer is kept awake while
any ticked agent of the same user on this computer is running a turn, and for a
grace period after the last one stops (a minute unless changed in \fBKeep awake
after agents stop\fR or with \fBagents grace\fR); the rest of the time they hold
nothing and the
computer sleeps on its usual timers. They are never turned off for you, and
they are remembered across restarts. An agent sitting at its prompt does not
count. The working sessions are listed in the tray menu and by
\fBwakeful status\fR.
.PP
Each agent is read every five seconds while it matters, from files it keeps for
itself; none of them is a documented interface:
.TP
.B Claude Code
\fI$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions/*.json\fR (default \fI~/.claude/sessions\fR):
working while a session is busy or still running a shell after its turn. A
session waiting for an answer does not count, nor one whose process has gone.
A conversation moved to the background counts through the background
session's file, not the one it was moved from.
.TP
.B Codex
\fI$CODEX_HOME/sessions/**/rollout\-*.jsonl\fR (default \fI~/.codex\fR):
working while the last turn event is a start and a process that started before
that turn holds the thread's lock in \fI$CODEX_HOME/thread\-writer\-locks\fR.
Waiting for approval counts.
.TP
.B opencode
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/opencode/opencode.db\fR (and, for 2.x, \fIopencode\-*.db\fR or
\fB$OPENCODE_DB\fR), opened read\-only and only while an opencode process runs:
working while it has an unfinished step or a busy run, an opencode
process that started before that step runs in the session's directory or below
it, or as the 2.x background service (\fBopencode serve \-\-service\fR), and,
for 1.x, the session has been written in the last two hours. Waiting
for approval counts.
.PP
\fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR, \fBCODEX_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR and
\fBOPENCODE_DB\fR are taken from the environment of the running tray, not of
the shell that runs a command.
.SH CLOSING THE LID
Closing the lid is an explicit request to sleep, and no inhibition stops it.
The tray's \fBAlso ignore closing the lid\fR option instead sets the lid action
in \fI~/.config/powerdevilrc\fR to "Do nothing" for every power profile while
the computer is being kept awake, by the timer or by working agents, and puts
the previous values back when it stops. The previous values are written to
\fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/wakeful/lid\-restore.json\fR before anything is changed. If
.B wakeful
is killed, a watchdog process restores them; if the watchdog is also gone (for
example after a reboot), the next start of
.B wakeful
restores them. A value changed in System Settings in the meantime is left as
it is.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wakeful/wakeful.conf
Preferences: mode, last length, lid option, agentic checks, notifications.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/wakeful.desktop
Present when "Start at login" is on.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/wakeful/lid\-restore.json
The lid values to restore; present only while the lid is being ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 when the tray is not running or refused the command. 2 for a
duration or switch that cannot be parsed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd\-inhibit (1)
.PP
https://github.com/stuckj/wakeful
